   419	nonzero locus. A valid partner with nonzero `e_0` or `e_16` coordinate, or a failed identity in the retained proof
   420	record, falsifies the result. Evidence: [`partner-purity-result.json`](partner-purity-result.json).
   421	
   422	### Can canonical-representative local data be applied to every retained degeneration?
   423	
   424	The question matters because the non-compact stratum symmetry does not lift to kernels, and several retained paths
   425	end at exact points different from the canonical regularity representatives. The cheapest decisive probe groups paths
   426	by their literal boundary coefficients and recomputes the transverse germ at the selected points. Nine exact points
   427	support all 33 paths and reproduce every tested orbit and germ invariant. The result licenses point-specific local
   428	analysis, not kernel transport. A mismatched local invariant or a path outside the recomputed tangent cone falsifies
   429	the result. Evidence: [`path-supported-basepoints-result.json`](path-supported-basepoints-result.json).
   430	
   431	### Does one blow-up resolve every nullity-8 to nullity-12 kernel Gauss map?
   432	
   433	The question matters because a projective direction on which the first-order form vanishes carries additional
   434	higher-order limits. The cheapest decisive probe eliminates each tangent cone through the exact first-order form and
   435	measures the zero fiber. Eight incidences have no projective zero direction, while seven do. Three of those seven have
   436	an identically zero first-order map. Therefore one blow-up does not resolve the whole family. A nonempty degeneracy
   437	locus in any of the eight resolved rows, or an empty locus in a row classified exceptional, falsifies the result.
   438	Evidence: [`first-order-normal-cones-result.json`](first-order-normal-cones-result.json).
   439	
   440	### Which exceptional passages are invisible on their whole first-order tangent cone?
   441	
   442	The question matters because an isolated base direction needs a local correction, whereas an identically zero map
   443	needs a full second-order model. The cheapest decisive probe compares the first-order image dimension with the
   444	tangent-cone and zero-fiber dimensions. E8-bar, `X_+`, and `X_-` into `VB` have empty projective first-order images;
   445	their zero fibers equal their whole tangent cones. A nonzero effective form on any of those three cones falsifies the
   446	result. Evidence: [`first-order-normal-cones-result.json`](first-order-normal-cones-result.json).
   447	
   448	### Do the two E8-bar sheets collapse to one first-order image component?
   449	
   450	The question matters because a collapse would identify the sheetwise attaching data before higher-order corrections.
   451	The cheapest probe compares the minimal components of each two-sheet germ with the eliminated image. Along `V`,
   452	`V^a_+`, and `V^a_-`, both the germ and the first-order image have two minimal components. **Inferred:** the sheets
   453	therefore have distinct first-order image closures: the Zariski-continuous image of an irreducible sheet has
   454	irreducible closure, so two image components cannot come from one collapsed common closure. The statement does not
   455	exclude higher-order intersections or equality after adding exceptional limits. A radical replay yielding one image
   456	component, or a sheet mapping wholly into the other sheet’s image, falsifies the result. Evidence:
   457	[`first-order-normal-cones-result.json`](first-order-normal-cones-result.json) and the
   458	[regularity record](../a5-strata-regularity/README.md#theorem-6-transverse-germs).
   459	
   460	### Does a first-order degeneracy direction determine its higher-order plane?
   461	
   462	The question matters because a positive answer would make one normal blow-up sufficient after a pointwise correction.
   463	The cheapest decisive probe fixes the first coefficient and varies exact second coefficients. The `W8`-to-`VB`
   464	witnesses give different four-planes with three-dimensional intersection, so the answer is negative in general. The
   465	`Iso`-to-`VB` control pair gives the same plane, so second-jet dependence is not automatic. Equality of the two `W8`
   466	planes, or failure of either exact arc, falsifies the result. Evidence:
   467	[`schur-degeneracy-witnesses-result.json`](schur-degeneracy-witnesses-result.json).
   468	
