An exact coefficient check found a useful symmetry: replacing `t9` by `-t9` carries the supplied X-plus source ideal and generator list to X-minus, with fixed generator signs. One completed fiber calculation can therefore serve both cases over the recorded field.

The review is complete. I’m checking the final citations and keeping full-fiber certificates separate from degree-by-degree bounds.
codex
The best next step is **one X-sign calculation using global ideal membership, followed by a full Rees-fiber certificate**. The supplied X-plus and X-minus inputs are related by an exact sign substitution, so their expensive computations can be shared. Iso needs a completion argument beyond its quadratic relations. The Azure failures already distinguish artificial address-space exhaustion from genuine memory pressure.

The commands below are proposed runs. I performed read-only inspection and exact coefficient comparisons; I launched no acquisition, cloud job, package build, or long algebra computation.

**First fix the mathematical target**

Write
\[
A=(k[t]/J)_{\mathfrak m},\qquad I=(G_1,\ldots,G_s)A,\qquad
F(I)=\bigoplus_{d\ge0}I^d/\mathfrak m I^d.
\]
The requested algebraic object is \(\operatorname{Proj}F(I)\), with its embedding, dimension, degree, Hilbert series, and preferably components. A finite initial Hilbert-function segment supplies partial information. The brief explicitly requires that distinction. [purpose.md:23–29](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/purpose.md:23)

Two qualifications matter:

- **The supplied \(G\) represents kernel-map coordinates.** The producer constructs principal four-Pfaffians of a rank-four Schur form for nullity-eight sources. Consequently, the relevant blow-up center is the coordinate ideal \(I=(G)\). Identifying \(I\) with the ideal of the boundary orbit \(Z\), as the purpose note does, needs a separate equality proof. Replacing \(I\) by \(I(Z)\) or \(\mathfrak m\) generally changes the exceptional fiber. [nullity16_basepoints.py:283–294](~/worktrees/IBMQuantum/a5-kernel-attachments/docs/evidence/a5-kernel-attachments/nullity16_basepoints.py:283), [purpose.md:12–20](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/purpose.md:12)
- **A completed calculation over \(\mathbb F_{32009}\) certifies that reduction.** The original producer distinguishes modular calculations from calculations over \(\mathbb Q(i)\). Agreement at several primes supports reconstruction; complex component claims require characteristic-zero verification or a suitable specialization argument. [nullity16_rees_fibers.py:119–123](~/worktrees/IBMQuantum/a5-kernel-attachments/docs/evidence/a5-kernel-attachments/nullity16_rees_fibers.py:119), [nullity16_rees_fibers.py:634–650](~/worktrees/IBMQuantum/a5-kernel-attachments/docs/evidence/a5-kernel-attachments/nullity16_rees_fibers.py:634)

**What the retained failures establish**

The product and module counts below follow directly from the displayed generator lists and loops.

| Run | Actual computation | Cost boundary and retained result |
|---|---|---|
| X-plus → N-plus | Degree-two and degree-three relations of \(F(I)\); 15 generators, seven variables. The cubic stage uses \(680\) products and \(8(680)+12=5,452\) module generators. | The local degree-two syzygy finishes with \(h_2=51\); the following stage exhausts memory. [input:8–20](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/kaggle-pack-a/xp_np/input.sing:8), [log:1–9](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/kaggle-pack-a/xp_np/singular.log:1) |
| X-minus → N-minus | Computes quadratic relations \(K_2\), then decomposes \(V(K_2)\). | The retained result is 69 quadrics, projective dimension three, degree twelve. The timeout occurs after those outputs, at `minAssGTZ`. [input:15–20](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/kaggle-pack-a/xm_nm/input.sing:15), [log:1–7](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/kaggle-pack-a/xm_nm/singular.log:1) |
| Iso \(K_2\) | Decomposes the ideal generated by 68 quadrics in 17 variables. | Its standard basis and Hilbert counts finish quickly; the difficult stage is minimal-prime decomposition. Degree 23 is the **degree of the quadratic scheme**, rather than the degree of its relations. [input:2–6](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/kaggle-pack-a/iso_k2/input.sing:2), [probe:1–8](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/probes/pD_iso.log:1) |
| E8-bar → N-plus, msolve | Eliminates the Rees parameter \(a\) from a nonhomogeneous graph with 31 variables and 37 equations. | The address-space-capped runs fail allocating exponent or column tables. Subsequent cgroup runs pass those allocation points and encounter genuine memory limits entering degree seven. [input:1–19](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/inputs/e8_np.ms:1), [flags:164–168](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/runbooks/compute-flags.md:164) |
| W8 → N-minus | Local degree-two syzygies for 24 generators and seven variables: \(300\) products and \(8(300)+1=2,401\) module generators. | Memory exhaustion occurs before \(h_2\). Singular reports about \(83.85\) million KiB allocated. [input:8–14](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/inputs/w8_nm_fiber.sing:8), [log:1–7](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/azure-vm2/results/w8_nm_fiber.log:1) |

Coefficient growth cannot explain these particular failures: their arithmetic is already over a fixed prime field. The expensive objects are polynomial supports, standard-basis intermediates, syzygy modules, and msolve’s monomial tables.

**Two exact reformulations shared by the difficult cases**

**A. Global ideal membership and coefficient rank**

For degree-\(d\) products \(P_1,\ldots,P_c\), form
\[
Q_d=J+\mathfrak m(P_1,\ldots,P_c).
\]
Then
\[
h_d=\dim_k\operatorname{span}\{\operatorname{NF}_{Q_d}(P_j)\}.
\]
The constant left kernel of their coefficient matrix supplies the degree-\(d\) relations.

The justification is exact: \(((P)+J)/Q_d\) is annihilated by \(\mathfrak m\), so localization at \(\mathfrak m\) preserves it. A global `dp` computation therefore recovers the same degree-\(d\) invariant as the local syzygy computation.

The existing X-minus probe already builds this quotient and completes `std(Q)` in seventeen recorded seconds. Its log separately records the subsequent reductions. The seventeen-second figure times the standard-basis stage, rather than a retained end-to-end coefficient-rank benchmark. [probe script:5–20](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/probes/pG_xm_global_d2.sing:5), [probe log:6–9](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/probes/pG_xm_global_d2.log:6)

Once certified relations \(K_{\le d-1}\) exist, use
`kbase(std(Kprevious),d)` as product representatives. Nakayama’s lemma justifies the reduction **locally**, which is sufficient for \(F(I)\). The retained counts give:

- X: \(680\) cubic monomials reduce to \(122\).
- Iso: \(969\) cubic monomials reduce to \(265\).

[probes/pA_xm_rel.log:9–13](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/probes/pA_xm_rel.log:9), [probes/pD_iso.log:3–6](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/probes/pD_iso.log:3)

**B. Pivot saturation for the entire Rees ideal**

Choose \(g=G_q\) that is a nonzerodivisor modulo \(J\). In \(k[t,y]\), define
\[
H=J+(g y_j-G_jy_q:j\ne q).
\]
Then
\[
\mathcal R(I)=k[t,y]/(H:g^\infty).
\]

After inverting \(g\), the equations express every \(y_j\) through \(y_q\). The nonzerodivisor condition makes contraction recover the Rees kernel. Thus
\[
F(I)=k[t,y]\big/\big((H:g^\infty)+(t)\big).
\]

This formulation avoids explicitly computing the large local syzygy module and avoids presenting the graph through \(a\). Saturation can still be expensive, but a completed result supplies the **full** fiber ideal and Hilbert series.

The installed Macaulay2 implementation exposes the same saturation mechanism and explicitly requires a suitable nonzerodivisor. [ReesAlgebra.m2:146–161](/usr/share/Macaulay2/ReesAlgebra.m2:146)

**1. X-plus → N-plus**

The strongest available reduction is an exact symmetry of the supplied inputs.

I checked every printed coefficient under
\[
\sigma(t_1,t_2,t_4,t_5,t_6,t_7,t_9)
=(t_1,t_2,t_4,t_5,t_6,t_7,-t_9).
\]
The identities over \(\mathbb F_{32009}\) are
\[
J^-_j=\epsilon_j\,J^+_j\circ\sigma,\qquad
\epsilon=(+,-,-,-,-,-,-,-,+,+,+,+),
\]
and
\[
G^-_j=\delta_j\,G^+_j\circ\sigma,\qquad
\delta=(+,+,+,+,+,+,-,-,-,-,-,-,-,-,-).
\]
These identities use the supplied generator order. [positive input:4–5](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/kaggle-pack-a/xp_np/input.sing:4), [negative input:4–5](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/kaggle-pack-a/xm_nm/input.sing:4)

Consequently, the two recorded fiber algebras are isomorphic, with target substitution
\[
y_j\longmapsto\delta_jy_j.
\]

Ranked options:

1. **Transport a completed X-minus fiber ideal through that substitution.** This preserves the full graded algebra. Transporting the existing quadratic ideal supplies only the corresponding quadratic upper approximation.
2. **Run A on one sign**, using the 122 normal cubic monomials, with multiplication reduced modulo \(J\) after every factor.
3. **Run B on that same sign** to close the full ideal. Transfer the result to the other sign.

The cubic probe fails before printing its product summary, hence before constructing \(Q_3\) or running `std(Q3)`. Its eager `phi(kb)` expansion is therefore the first mechanism to change. [pH script:12–18](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/probes/pH_xm_global_d3.sing:12), [pH log:1–7](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/probes/pH_xm_global_d3.log:1)

Start with **16 GiB, one thread, one hour** for the reduced cubic calculation. Treat that duration as a budget, not a completion estimate.

Falsifiers:

- Any failed source-generator identity invalidates symmetry transport.
- The raw degree-two calculation must return \(h_2=51\).
- A correct cubic calculation must satisfy \(h_3\le122\).
- A value \(h_3<122\) disproves quadratic completeness.
- A value \(h_3=122\) establishes equality only through degree three.

Changing to the ordinary tangent cone of \(Y\) changes the question unless the kernel-coordinate map extends across every exceptional direction and its image is proved equal to the required limit set.

**2. X-minus → N-minus**

Separate the inexpensive support question for \(K_2\) from the harder completeness question for \(F(I)\).

The probes show
\[
y_1^2,y_5^2,y_6^2\in K_2.
\]
Adding \(y_1,y_5,y_6\) preserves the reduced support. After that addition, `minAssChar` returns one minimal prime with projective dimension three and degree twelve in one recorded second. [pB log:3–5](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/probes/pB_xm_decomp.log:3), [pC script:5–13](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/probes/pC_xm_reduce.sing:5), [pC log:1–5](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/probes/pC_xm_reduce.log:1)

Ranked options:

1. **Use the nilpotent-coordinate reduction plus `minAssChar`** to finish the arithmetic support description of \(V(K_2)\). Retain its prime generators.
2. **Use A degree by degree** to seek missing relations cheaply.
3. **Use B** to certify the entire fiber algebra.

A useful alternative completion argument is geometric: if the candidate support is irreducible and a certified family of limiting kernel planes has the same dimension and dense image in that support, the support equality follows. A few retained arcs establish containment, rather than density.

Use **2–4 GiB and ten minutes** for the existing reduced decomposition; use **16 GiB and one hour** for each full-fiber experiment.

Falsifiers:

- The unreduced quadratic quotient must reproduce \(15,51,122,240,417\) in degrees one through five. [pA log:9–13](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/probes/pA_xm_rel.log:9)
- Adding the three linear coordinates must preserve reduced support. Their square-membership certificates justify that assertion.
- Every claimed limiting arc must satisfy the final support ideal.
- Extra cubic or higher relations can shrink \(V(K_2)\), even after its decomposition succeeds.

Adding those linear coordinates preserves the **set** and changes the graded scheme. Keep the original quadratic ideal for Hilbert-function claims.

**3. Iso \(K_2\)**

The input has 68 quadrics, and its quotient has Hilbert values \(17,85,265,640\). The direct `minAssGTZ` run times out; the alternative `minAssChar` probe also times out. A single `facstd` piece establishes only that this factorization attempt produced one piece. [pD log:1–8](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/probes/pD_iso.log:1), [pE log:1](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/probes/pE_iso_char.log:1)

Two equivalent ways to pursue the required fiber are:

1. **A with the 265 normal cubic representatives**, followed by higher-degree calculations and an independent completion certificate.
2. **B from the original 17 generators and quadric \(J\)**, producing the whole special fiber directly.

For decomposition of a certified target ideal, use **projective affine charts** as an alternative to one global primary decomposition:

- Set \(y_j=1\), eliminate variables that become linearly solvable, and decompose the smaller affine ideal.
- Homogenize and contract each component, including saturation by the chart coordinate.
- Cover every remaining projective chart and remove duplicate components.
- Compare the reconstructed support ideal with the radical of the saturated target ideal.

The chart cover preserves projective support. Reconstructing the full chart ideals preserves the projective scheme; retaining only their primes discards nilpotent structure.

Start A with **16 GiB, one thread, one hour**. Give individual chart experiments **8 GiB and fifteen minutes**, retaining completed chart results. These budgets bound investigation; their success remains unmeasured.

Falsifiers:

- Raw degree two must give \(h_2=85\).
- Cubic \(h_3\) must satisfy \(h_3\le265\).
- A decomposition of \(K_2\) must reproduce its saturated support and account for scheme multiplicities where degree 23 is used.
- A component omitted from the chosen chart cover invalidates the decomposition.

The sister analysis’s reduction from 969 to 265 products is sound. Its suggestion to stop a truncation after two stable ranks lacks a completeness bound. [algebra-options.md:27–30](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-iso-np-h3-analysis/algebra-options.md:27)

**4. Azure: E8-bar → N-plus and W8 → N-minus**

For E8-bar, the latest retained measurements already refine the diagnosis:

- Address-space-capped runs fail in exponent or column-table allocation.
- A cgroup run passes those earlier failure points.
- Native msolve 0.11.0 then encounters a genuine cgroup memory limit entering degree seven.

[compute-flags.md:164–168](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/runbooks/compute-flags.md:164)

The line `7 407116 432548` records **degree, selected pairs, and remaining pairs**. Its matrix dimensions have not yet been printed. A preceding degree-six matrix is explicitly \(241740\times516909\). [msolve log:23–31](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/azure-vm2/results/e8_np_msolve_v011.log:23), [degree-six trace:85–97](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/azure-vm2/results/e8_np_msolve_v011.log:85)

For both Azure targets, rank the alternatives as follows:

1. **A in the source ring.** E8-bar has 231 quadratic products in nine variables; W8 has 300 in seven variables. This removes the large local module and avoids the full Rees graph during the initial measurement.
2. **B with a sparse nonzerodivisor pivot.** This is the next full-fiber attempt.
3. **Certified sparse Macaulay linear algebra**, described below, if standard-basis memory remains excessive.
4. **A larger-memory msolve replay** as a bounded comparison experiment.

Begin E8-bar A with **16 GiB and 45 minutes**, and W8 A with **16 GiB and 30 minutes**. A capped failure supplies a measured basis for choosing the next substrate. An approved VM run can then test **64 GiB, one expensive job at a time**.

For W8, the original script already shows genuine large resident allocation before producing \(h_2\). Raising the address-space ceiling alone cannot remove that module-growth mechanism. [w8 log:1–7](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/azure-vm2/results/w8_nm_fiber.log:1)

Falsifiers:

- Compute \(h_1\) first. A rank below the supplied generator count exposes further local generator redundancy.
- Every recovered homogeneous relation must pass exact membership in \(J+\mathfrak m I^d\).
- Completed Rees methods must agree on dimension, degree, and tested Hilbert values.
- A cgroup OOM event with unrestricted address space identifies a physical-memory budget failure; an allocation error far below the cgroup limit requires renewed allocator and virtual-memory investigation.

A tangent-cone replacement alone computes a different object. The recorded germ dimensions provide useful upper bounds:
\[
\dim\operatorname{Proj}F(I)\le
\begin{cases}
3&X,\\
4&\mathrm{Iso},\ \overline{E8},\\
5&W8.
\end{cases}
\]
The corresponding source-germ dimensions are four, five, five, and six. [regularity README:261–273](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/evidence/a5-strata-regularity/README.md:261)

**Concrete reusable scripts**

For method A, load the existing header through the `ideal G=...;` line, changing `ds` to `dp`. Use five header lines for X, E8-bar, and W8, and three for Iso. Set `fiberDegree` and `previousRelationFile` before this body; use an empty string for the initial raw calculation.

```singular
LIB "matrix.lib";
option(redSB);

int generatorCount=ncols(G);
int sourceVariableCount=nvars(basering);
def sourceRing=basering;
ideal sourceBasis=std(J);

int generatorIndex;
for(generatorIndex=1;generatorIndex<=generatorCount;generatorIndex++)
{
  G[generatorIndex]=reduce(G[generatorIndex],sourceBasis);
}

execute("ring targetRing=32009,(y(1.."+string(generatorCount)+")),dp;");
ideal previousRelations;
if(size(previousRelationFile)>0)
{
  execute("previousRelations="+read(previousRelationFile)+";");
}
ideal candidateMonomials=kbase(std(previousRelations),fiberDegree);
int candidateCount=ncols(candidateMonomials);

setring sourceRing;
ideal products;
int productIndex;
int factorIndex;
int factorPower;
intvec exponentVector;
poly currentProduct;

for(productIndex=1;productIndex<=candidateCount;productIndex++)
{
  setring targetRing;
  exponentVector=leadexp(candidateMonomials[productIndex]);
  setring sourceRing;
  currentProduct=1;
  for(factorIndex=1;factorIndex<=generatorCount;factorIndex++)
  {
    for(factorPower=1;factorPower<=exponentVector[factorIndex];factorPower++)
    {
      currentProduct=reduce(currentProduct*G[factorIndex],sourceBasis);
    }
  }
  products[productIndex]=currentProduct;
}

ideal denominatorBasis=std(sourceBasis+maxideal(1)*products);
vector normalVector;
poly variableProduct=1;
for(factorIndex=1;factorIndex<=sourceVariableCount;factorIndex++)
{
  variableProduct=variableProduct*var(factorIndex);
}
for(productIndex=1;productIndex<=candidateCount;productIndex++)
{
  normalVector=normalVector
    +reduce(products[productIndex],denominatorBasis)*gen(productIndex);
}

matrix rawCoefficients,coefficientMonomials;
coef(normalVector,variableProduct,rawCoefficients,coefficientMonomials);
matrix coefficients[candidateCount][ncols(rawCoefficients)];
int coefficientRow;
int coefficientColumn;
for(coefficientRow=1;coefficientRow<=nrows(rawCoefficients);coefficientRow++)
{
  for(coefficientColumn=1;coefficientColumn<=ncols(rawCoefficients);coefficientColumn++)
  {
    coefficients[coefficientRow,coefficientColumn]
      =rawCoefficients[coefficientRow,coefficientColumn];
  }
}
int fiberRank=rank(coefficients);
"RESULT degree",fiberDegree;
"RESULT h",fiberRank;
matrix relationVectors=syz(transpose(coefficients));

setring targetRing;
matrix targetRelations=imap(sourceRing,relationVectors);
ideal newRelations;
int relationIndex;
poly relationPolynomial;
for(relationIndex=1;relationIndex<=ncols(targetRelations);relationIndex++)
{
  relationPolynomial=0;
  for(productIndex=1;productIndex<=candidateCount;productIndex++)
  {
    relationPolynomial=relationPolynomial
      +targetRelations[productIndex,relationIndex]*candidateMonomials[productIndex];
  }
  newRelations[relationIndex]=relationPolynomial;
}
ideal cumulativeRelations=std(previousRelations+newRelations);
write(":w fiber-degree-"+string(fiberDegree)+".rel",cumulativeRelations);
quit;
```

The vector coefficient interface retains product-slot identities, including zero normal forms. The installed manual documents that interface and its coefficient/monomial matrices. [Singular coef manual:73–100](/usr/share/doc/singular/sing_224.htm:73)

For method B, append this body to the same global header:

```singular
LIB "elim.lib";
LIB "primdec.lib";
option(redSB);

int sourceVariableCount=nvars(basering);
int generatorCount=ncols(G);
string sourceNames=varstr(basering);
def sourceRing=basering;

execute("ring reesRing=32009,("+sourceNames
  +",y(1.."+string(generatorCount)+")),dp;");
ideal sourceRelations=std(imap(sourceRing,J));
ideal sourceGenerators=imap(sourceRing,G);

int pivotIndex=1;
poly pivotGenerator=sourceGenerators[pivotIndex];
ideal pivotColon=quotient(sourceRelations,ideal(pivotGenerator));
if(size(reduce(pivotColon,sourceRelations))!=0)
{
  ERROR("the chosen pivot is a zero divisor modulo J");
}

ideal equations=sourceRelations;
ideal baseMaximal;
ideal targetMaximal;
int variableIndex;
for(variableIndex=1;variableIndex<=sourceVariableCount;variableIndex++)
{
  baseMaximal=baseMaximal,var(variableIndex);
}
for(variableIndex=1;variableIndex<=generatorCount;variableIndex++)
{
  targetMaximal=targetMaximal,var(sourceVariableCount+variableIndex);
  if(variableIndex!=pivotIndex)
  {
    equations=equations,
      pivotGenerator*var(sourceVariableCount+variableIndex)
      -sourceGenerators[variableIndex]*var(sourceVariableCount+pivotIndex);
  }
}

ideal reesIdeal=sat(equations,ideal(pivotGenerator));
ideal specialFiber=std(reesIdeal+baseMaximal);
"RESULT fiber_projective_dimension",dim(specialFiber)-1;
"RESULT fiber_degree",mult(specialFiber);
"RESULT fiber_hilbert_numerator",hilb(specialFiber,1);
write(":w special-fiber.sing",specialFiber);

ideal projectiveFiber=sat(specialFiber,targetMaximal);
write(":w projective-fiber.sing",projectiveFiber);
list components=minAssChar(projectiveFiber,0);
"RESULT component_count",size(components);
for(variableIndex=1;variableIndex<=size(components);variableIndex++)
{
  ideal componentBasis=std(components[variableIndex]);
  "RESULT component",variableIndex,dim(componentBasis)-1,mult(componentBasis);
  write(":w component-"+string(variableIndex)+".sing",componentBasis);
  kill componentBasis;
}
quit;
```

Keep the original `specialFiber` for Hilbert-function claims. Saturation by target coordinates preserves the projective scheme while potentially changing low-degree graded pieces. In Singular 4.4, `sat` returns an ideal; indexing it with `[1]` selects a generator. [elim.lib:717–726](/usr/share/singular/LIB/elim.lib:717)

Run each recipe from a separate scratch directory. A host launch is:

```sh
systemd-run --user --scope \
  -p MemoryMax=16G -p MemorySwapMax=0 \
  env MALLOC_ARENA_MAX=2 \
      OMP_NUM_THREADS=1 OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS=1 GOROROBA_WORKERS=1 \
      GLIBC_TUNABLES=glibc.rtld.optional_static_tls=4096 \
  timeout -k 10 3600 \
  Singular -q --no-rc --cpus=1 --threads=1 --flint-threads=1 input.sing \
  > run.log 2>&1
```

Record the exit status, cgroup memory peak/events, input hashes, binary version, and complete stage timings. The runbook requires the memory controller and flag review before execution; its Singular section explains why extra thread flags do not parallelize one `std` or `syz`. [compute-flags.md:51–65](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/runbooks/compute-flags.md:51), [compute-flags.md:125–144](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/runbooks/compute-flags.md:125), [checklist:179–196](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/runbooks/compute-flags.md:179)

An optional msolve comparison on an **approved, otherwise available VM** is:

```sh
ulimit -v unlimited
systemd-run --user --scope \
  -p MemoryMax=64G -p MemorySwapMax=0 \
  env MALLOC_ARENA_MAX=2 OMP_PLACES=cores OMP_PROC_BIND=close \
  timeout -k 10 3600 \
  msolve -t 2 -s 24 -u 2 -l 2 -e 1 -g 2 -v 2 \
