**Two exact reformulations shared by the difficult cases**

**A. Global ideal membership and coefficient rank**

For degree-\(d\) products \(P_1,\ldots,P_c\), form
\[
Q_d=J+\mathfrak m(P_1,\ldots,P_c).
\]
Then
\[
h_d=\dim_k\operatorname{span}\{\operatorname{NF}_{Q_d}(P_j)\}.
\]
The constant left kernel of their coefficient matrix supplies the degree-\(d\) relations.

The justification is exact: \(((P)+J)/Q_d\) is annihilated by \(\mathfrak m\), so localization at \(\mathfrak m\) preserves it. A global `dp` computation therefore recovers the same degree-\(d\) invariant as the local syzygy computation.

The existing X-minus probe already builds this quotient and completes `std(Q)` in seventeen recorded seconds. Its log separately records the subsequent reductions. The seventeen-second figure times the standard-basis stage, rather than a retained end-to-end coefficient-rank benchmark. [probe script:5–20](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/probes/pG_xm_global_d2.sing:5), [probe log:6–9](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/probes/pG_xm_global_d2.log:6)

Once certified relations \(K_{\le d-1}\) exist, use
`kbase(std(Kprevious),d)` as product representatives. Nakayama’s lemma justifies the reduction **locally**, which is sufficient for \(F(I)\). The retained counts give:

- X: \(680\) cubic monomials reduce to \(122\).
- Iso: \(969\) cubic monomials reduce to \(265\).

[probes/pA_xm_rel.log:9–13](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/probes/pA_xm_rel.log:9), [probes/pD_iso.log:3–6](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/probes/pD_iso.log:3)

**B. Pivot saturation for the entire Rees ideal**

Choose \(g=G_q\) that is a nonzerodivisor modulo \(J\). In \(k[t,y]\), define
\[
H=J+(g y_j-G_jy_q:j\ne q).
\]
Then
\[
\mathcal R(I)=k[t,y]/(H:g^\infty).
\]

After inverting \(g\), the equations express every \(y_j\) through \(y_q\). The nonzerodivisor condition makes contraction recover the Rees kernel. Thus
\[
F(I)=k[t,y]\big/\big((H:g^\infty)+(t)\big).
\]

This formulation avoids explicitly computing the large local syzygy module and avoids presenting the graph through \(a\). Saturation can still be expensive, but a completed result supplies the **full** fiber ideal and Hilbert series.

The installed Macaulay2 implementation exposes the same saturation mechanism and explicitly requires a suitable nonzerodivisor. [ReesAlgebra.m2:146–161](/usr/share/Macaulay2/ReesAlgebra.m2:146)

**1. X-plus → N-plus**

The strongest available reduction is an exact symmetry of the supplied inputs.

I checked every printed coefficient under
\[
\sigma(t_1,t_2,t_4,t_5,t_6,t_7,t_9)
=(t_1,t_2,t_4,t_5,t_6,t_7,-t_9).
\]
The identities over \(\mathbb F_{32009}\) are
\[
J^-_j=\epsilon_j\,J^+_j\circ\sigma,\qquad
\epsilon=(+,-,-,-,-,-,-,-,+,+,+,+),
\]
and
\[
G^-_j=\delta_j\,G^+_j\circ\sigma,\qquad
\delta=(+,+,+,+,+,+,-,-,-,-,-,-,-,-,-).
\]
These identities use the supplied generator order. [positive input:4–5](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/kaggle-pack-a/xp_np/input.sing:4), [negative input:4–5](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/kaggle-pack-a/xm_nm/input.sing:4)

Consequently, the two recorded fiber algebras are isomorphic, with target substitution
\[
y_j\longmapsto\delta_jy_j.
\]

Ranked options:

1. **Transport a completed X-minus fiber ideal through that substitution.** This preserves the full graded algebra. Transporting the existing quadratic ideal supplies only the corresponding quadratic upper approximation.
2. **Run A on one sign**, using the 122 normal cubic monomials, with multiplication reduced modulo \(J\) after every factor.
3. **Run B on that same sign** to close the full ideal. Transfer the result to the other sign.

The cubic probe fails before printing its product summary, hence before constructing \(Q_3\) or running `std(Q3)`. Its eager `phi(kb)` expansion is therefore the first mechanism to change. [pH script:12–18](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/probes/pH_xm_global_d3.sing:12), [pH log:1–7](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/probes/pH_xm_global_d3.log:1)

Start with **16 GiB, one thread, one hour** for the reduced cubic calculation. Treat that duration as a budget, not a completion estimate.

